think about how many conclusions people  have come to throughout history that  have eventually been proven false  despite a majority of society agreeing  with the conclusions at the time they  were made this brings us to our next  fallacy appeal to the people or as it's  also known in Latin argumentum ad  populum this is an argument that  concludes that something is true or  false because many are most people  believe that the conclusion is true or  false this fallacy is also known as the  bandwagon fallacy an appeal to the  masses or the consensus fallacy do we  know that Christianity is true because  it has more adherents in the world than  any other religion do we know that a  political candidate was the best choice  in an election because they won the  popular vote do we know that a musician  has talent because their album sales are  through the roof the premise is most of  society believes X conclusion X is true  if this is meant to be a deductive  argument this argument is invalid  because the premise is irrelevant to the  conclusion something can be true even if  many or most people believe it to be  false and vice versa  the same thing would apply to a  deductive argument like this one most  people do X therefore X is the best way  to achieve Y as a deductive argument the  conclusion does not follow but an appeal  to the people can sometimes be valid in  an inductive argument for example if a  poll with a large population size is  taken in a certain city and the poll  finds that ninety-nine point seven  percent of people want their City  Council to take a certain measure on a  certain issue you could use that  premises and evidence to inductively  argue that the next person from that  area to be asked the same question will  probably side with the majority here  what most people believe actually does  have bearing on the trueness of the  conclusion but for instance it would be  fallacious to use that as proof  deductively that  what those 99.7% of people wan